faq:
  - q: "What is the best way to research salary?"
    a: "Use multiple data sources and triangulate. No single source is accurate — each has biases and gaps. The best approach: Levels.fyi for tech roles (self-reported, highly specific), Glassdoor for broad industry coverage, LinkedIn Salary for role-specific ranges by location, Payscale and Radford (if you have access) for corporate benchmarks, and active job postings that list salary ranges (required in California, Colorado, New York, and other states). Three or more sources pointing to the same range = reliable data."
  - q: "How accurate is Glassdoor salary data?"
    a: "Moderately accurate as a directional benchmark — not as a precise number. Glassdoor data is self-reported and may skew toward unhappy employees sharing low numbers or outliers sharing high ones. It is most reliable for large-company roles with many data points. For small companies or niche roles, Glassdoor data is less reliable. Use it as one data point alongside LinkedIn Salary and role-specific sources like Levels.fyi."
  - q: "How do I find out what my coworkers earn?"
    a: "In the United States, you have a legal right to discuss wages with coworkers under the National Labor Relations Act. Many professionals don't know this. Direct salary conversations with trusted colleagues are the most accurate data source available — they account for your specific company, level, and location. The conversation can be initiated simply: 'I'm trying to make sure I'm at market. Do you mind sharing what you're at?'"
  - q: "How do I use job postings to research salary?"
    a: "Many states now require companies to post salary ranges in job descriptions (California, Colorado, New York, Washington, and others). Filter job searches for your role and level in your market and look for postings that include salary ranges. Even postings without listed ranges can be researched — apply for the role or reach out to the recruiter and ask about the range before interviewing."
  - q: "What information do I need to research salary accurately?"
    a: "Four variables: (1) Role title — exact title, not approximate. 'Senior Software Engineer' and 'Software Engineer' have different market rates. (2) Level — where you are in the seniority ladder at your target company. (3) Location or remote status — salaries vary significantly by market. San Francisco Senior Engineers earn 20–40% more than the national median. (4) Company stage and size — startups vs. public companies vs. large enterprises pay differently for the same role."
